 ## Background
-The original NeRF architecture, introduced by Mildenhall et al. in 2020, established the baseline MLP-based radiance field representation [S2]. Subsequent research rapidly expanded the architectural landscape to address limitations in training speed, generalization, geometric accuracy, and single-view synthesis. Methods like AdaNeRF and ProNeRF focused on efficiency and sparse sampling, while others like MultiPlaneNeRF and RING-NeRF introduced structural inductive biases for better generalization and LOD. Generative approaches like NeRDi and DiffusioNeRF integrated pre-trained diffusion models, and geometric-focused methods like AligNeRF and DE-NeRF enhanced structural fidelity. The field continues to evolve with hybrid architectures combining transformers, diffusion models, and sparse representations.
+The original NeRF paper [S2] introduced the basic MLP architecture. Subsequent research has rapidly expanded this into a diverse family of architectures addressing specific challenges such as generalization [S1, unofficial], efficiency [S1, unofficial], geometry [S3], and dynamics [S5].
 
